1
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:07,070
♪ In the not too distant future

2
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:09,070
♪ Next Sunday, AD

3
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:13,070
♪ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,580
♪ Not too different
from you or me

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:19,580
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:23,090
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:25,590
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place

8
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:27,090
♪ But his bosses didn't like 'im

9
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:30,100
♪ So they shot him into space

10
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:32,090
(rocket launching)

11
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:35,590
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,100
♪ The worst we can find

13
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,100
♪ La la la

14
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:41,110
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all

15
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:44,100
♪ And we'll monitor his mind

16
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:45,610
♪ La la la

17
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:47,610
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control

18
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:49,610
♪ Where the movies begin or end

19
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:51,120
♪ La la la

20
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:54,110
♪ Because he used
those special parts

21
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,620
♪ To make his robot friends

22
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,630
♪ Robot roll call

23
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,120
♪ Cambot

24
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,120
♪ Gypsy

25
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:04,630
♪ Tom Servo

26
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:06,630
♪ Crow

27
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:09,630
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

28
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,640
♪ And other science facts

29
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:12,630
♪ La la la

30
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,140
♪ Then repeat to yourself

31
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:16,140
♪ It's just a show

32
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,640
♪ I should really just relax

33
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:24,650
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000

34
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:29,150
(metal sliding and clanging)

35
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:36,420
Hi, welcome to the
Satellite of Love.

36
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:37,430
My name's Joel,

37
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:39,430
and I've fashioned
my two robot friends,

38
00:01:39,430 --> 00:01:40,930
Tom and Crow,

39
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:41,930
into an at-home
shooting gallery.

40
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:43,940
It does get boring up here.

41
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:45,930
Stop talking and
start chalking.

42
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:47,430
(gun firing, bullet pinging)

43
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,940
As you can probably tell
from the opening the Mads made,

44
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:51,940
I'm marooned up here in space,

45
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:53,950
without much chance of
ever really getting home.

46
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:56,940
Something is wrong on Saturn 3

47
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:58,450
(gun firing, bullet pinging)

48
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:00,450
Anyway, they
monitor our responses

49
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:02,450
to watching really bad movies,

50
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:05,460
and then they sell the
results to cable TV.

51
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:06,950
Five seconds to
commercial sign.

52
00:02:07,460 --> 00:02:09,460
Say, partner, that's some
mighty fancy shootin'.

53
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,460
(gun firing, bullet pinging)
Thank you.

54
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:13,460
Commercial sign in five

55
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:14,970
four, three, two.

56
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:16,460
Commercial sign now.

57
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:17,970
We'll be right back.

58
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:20,970
(gun firing repeatedly,
bullets pinging)

59
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:26,780
(MST3K theme music)

60
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,280
Hey everybody, look,

61
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:31,280
I changed the robots to
Apple Dumpling Gang mode.

62
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,280
Draw, Tim Conway!

63
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:34,790
Uh, er, Jeepers!

64
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:35,780
Sorry about getting that

65
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:37,780
ketchup on your
scarf, Mr. Black Bart.

66
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:38,790
Can I call you Bart?

67
00:02:38,790 --> 00:02:40,290
Is that okay with you?

68
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:42,290
Uh, gee, I'll get some soda
water and clean that right up.

69
00:02:42,290 --> 00:02:44,800
(gun firing, bullet pinging)
Ow, ow, ow, ow.

70
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:46,290
Now, your turn, Don Knotts.

71
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,800
I'll have you know,

72
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,300
these hands have to be
registered as lethal weapons

73
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,300
with the Mount Pilot
police department.

74
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,310
And when in Los Angeles,
visit Universal Studios.

75
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:57,800
(gun firing, bullet pinging)

76
00:02:57,810 --> 00:02:59,310
Oh, you guys, wake up!

77
00:02:59,310 --> 00:03:00,810
The Mads are calling.

78
00:03:00,810 --> 00:03:02,310
Come on, get up!

79
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:05,310
Hi!

80
00:03:05,310 --> 00:03:09,310
Hello, evil over,
under, over lords.

81
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:11,320
Oh, shut your
mouth, Pollyanna Prod,

82
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,320
and show us your
invention exchange.

83
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:15,330
And don't dawdle, Amaryllis.

84
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:16,320
Do you mean me?

85
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:19,330
No, him, Beefaroni.

86
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:21,830
Well, sirs, today's
invention exchange

87
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:23,330
is based on the old premise

88
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,840
of this age-old
gag, the bang gun.

89
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:28,840
It's not that great.

90
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:30,340
Not really that much fun.

91
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:34,350
So, I took that premise, and
based it on this new bang Uzi.

92
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:38,340
See, you can point it
directly at someone,

93
00:03:38,350 --> 00:03:40,350
and if you do it right.

94
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:41,850
Real arcade action!

95
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:43,350
Gimme two!

96
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:44,860
- Well?
- Kaboom.

97
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:46,350
Ah, very funny.

98
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:47,360
We can do better than that.

99
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:49,360
In fact, we've come up
with this harpoon gun

100
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:50,860
with appropriate noise.

101
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:51,860
Start running, Frank.

102
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:53,870
Oh, I hate this one!

103
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:57,360
Shh-toonk (vibrating sound).

104
00:03:57,370 --> 00:03:59,370
Funny, huh? (exhales)

105
00:03:59,370 --> 00:04:00,870
Well, hey, sirs.

106
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:03,370
Here's a knife adapted
to the same purpose.

107
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:06,380
Shhhhlink?

108
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:07,880
Well, ball's in your court.

109
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:09,380
Ball's in our court?

110
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,380
I'll show you balls.

111
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,890
Frank has got some nun-chucks
using the same tired premise.

112
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:16,380
Hi-kee-ba!

113
00:04:18,390 --> 00:04:19,390
In-sa-no-bock.

114
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:21,390
Well, here's a
stick o'dynamite.

115
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:22,390
Just light the fuse.

116
00:04:22,390 --> 00:04:24,400
Oh, I can't hold my ears.

117
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:25,390
And...

118
00:04:25,890 --> 00:04:27,390
Ka-chow!

119
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:29,400
Ka-chow? Ka-chow?

120
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:32,400
What, is Don Martin
working with you guys now?

121
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:34,410
Frank, get the plunger ready.

122
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,900
Well, your experiment
this week, Joel,

123
00:04:36,910 --> 00:04:39,910
is a TV movie called
Stranded in Space.

124
00:04:39,910 --> 00:04:41,410
It's a real stinker.

125
00:04:41,410 --> 00:04:42,410
And, I'm sorry.

126
00:04:42,410 --> 00:04:44,920
But, if I don't do
it, someone else will.

127
00:04:44,910 --> 00:04:46,910
Finish him off, Frank.

128
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:52,420
Boingee-banga-kabonga?

129
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:55,930
(all exclaiming)

130
00:04:55,920 --> 00:05:00,920
(metal sliding and clanging)

131
00:05:05,830 --> 00:05:08,330
Now, was that
bingo-bango-kabongo?

132
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:09,840
That was weird.

133
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:10,840
Boingo-bango-ka. Oh.

134
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,340
Oh, make it stop!

135
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:14,350
It's being seen
through the eyes

136
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:16,840
of Yule Brenner
in Westworld here.

137
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:18,850
You know, already
this movie's like

138
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:19,850
goin' to the dentist.

139
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:20,850
(Tom chuckling)

140
00:05:20,850 --> 00:05:22,350
Yeah.

141
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:23,860
All right, your order is $5.25.

142
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:26,850
Please drive forward to the
first window, thank you.

143
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:29,860
Now, you know,

144
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:31,360
I don't think we should get
wrapped up in these credits.

145
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,870
I'm sure they have nothing
to do with the movie.

146
00:05:38,370 --> 00:05:39,870
Hmm, Shrinky Dinks!

147
00:05:41,370 --> 00:05:42,870
Wait a minute.

148
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:44,380
Oh, wait.

149
00:05:44,370 --> 00:05:47,870
I think I see Charles
Foster Kane's sled in there.

150
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:48,880
In Xanadu.

151
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:51,880
Time to start thinking
about me, Steve Franken.

152
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:54,390
Tim Conway, Dorf in Space?

153
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:55,880
Oh, Tim O'Connnor.

154
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:56,880
Sorry.

155
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:00,390
Woop!
Oh, damn roller-blades!

156
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:02,890
I can't stop it, I
don't know how it works.

157
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:04,400
Goodbye, folks!

158
00:06:04,390 --> 00:06:05,890
Goodbye!

159
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:12,400
Hmmm. Uh-oh.

160
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:16,400
Oh, that's bad luck

161
00:06:16,410 --> 00:06:17,910
- Three in a transporter?
- Yeah.

162
00:06:18,410 --> 00:06:19,410
Mmm-huh.

163
00:06:23,410 --> 00:06:24,920
Now, see,

164
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:25,910
what did that have
to do with the movie?

165
00:06:25,910 --> 00:06:27,410
Nothing.

166
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,420
You know, I just had
the craziest dream.

167
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:30,920
And you were there.
And you, Tim.

168
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,430
That one doesn't work anymore.

169
00:06:38,430 --> 00:06:39,930
Everything checks out okay.

170
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:41,930
And so do I.

171
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:46,440
Now, let's see if we
can strand this baby.

172
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:51,440
Houston, this is Patriot.

173
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:54,450
Reading you loud
and clear, Neil.

174
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,940
Steve's checked and
rechecked the transponder.

175
00:06:56,950 --> 00:07:00,450
We can't find anything
wrong with it on this end.

176
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:01,450
Roger.

177
00:07:01,450 --> 00:07:02,950
Looks like you'll
have to continue

178
00:07:02,950 --> 00:07:04,460
performing your correction burns

179
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:05,950
using your on-board computer.

180
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:08,450
And using those sideburns
we set ya up with.

181
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:09,960
Roger.

182
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:11,960
At least our computer seems
to be functioning okay.

183
00:07:11,960 --> 00:07:13,970
Oh, those are
the lotto numbers.

184
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,960
In case we can't locate
the problem down here,

185
00:07:16,970 --> 00:07:19,470
you should be close
enough in about seven days

186
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:20,970
for us to skin paint
you on our radar.

187
00:07:20,970 --> 00:07:22,470
- Skin paint?
- Roger.

188
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:23,980
Well, I'll try anything once.

189
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:25,470
After all this
time in deep space,

190
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:26,970
I guess we can
wait another week.

191
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:28,480
Good one.

192
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,980
Everything else
all right up there?

193
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:32,980
Aside from the fact we're
not a hundred percent sure

194
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:35,990
we're on the right trajectory,
everything's great.

195
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:37,480
And, we ran outta
oxygen two days ago.

196
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:38,990
We'll keep in touch.

197
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:40,990
We'll be here.

198
00:07:41,490 --> 00:07:42,990
So, whose turn was it?

199
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:45,990
Hey, it's I Dream of Jeannie.

200
00:07:45,990 --> 00:07:46,990
Oh!

201
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,500
Hey, was that us?

202
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,000
I never thought I'd consider
26,000 miles-an-hour slow.

203
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,510
Until I popped a Dexatrim.

204
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,000
You get the feeling Mike's
a little anxious to get home?

205
00:07:58,010 --> 00:08:00,010
Well, a new bride
will do that to you.

206
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:02,010
Especially when you
haven't had a chance

207
00:08:02,010 --> 00:08:03,510
to take her on a honeymoon.

208
00:08:03,510 --> 00:08:06,020
Don't worry, I'll make
up for it when I get back.

209
00:08:06,010 --> 00:08:07,510
And how!

210
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:09,520
He's right, folks.
Watch out for that one.

211
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:10,520
Whoa.

212
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,520
What's on the menu, Junior?

213
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:15,030
There's a real pretty sight.

214
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:18,020
- Where that transponder bailed.
- It's a Universal picture.

215
00:08:18,530 --> 00:08:20,530
I wasn't really
sure I'd see it again.

216
00:08:20,530 --> 00:08:22,030
That makes two of us.

217
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:23,530
Uh, that's three, Steve.

218
00:08:23,530 --> 00:08:25,540
Might carve out a
nice, thick, juicy--

219
00:08:25,530 --> 00:08:27,530
(explosion)
Seizure! Ah!

220
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:29,040
Set up our
communications line.

221
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:31,540
Was that it the food tube?

222
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:33,040
Try and get 'em reset,
Steve. (alarm blares)

223
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:34,550
I'll see if we still have
voice to Mission Control.

224
00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:36,040
Houston, this is
Patriot, do you read me?

225
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:37,540
Must be time for class.

226
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:38,550
Houston, this is patriot!

227
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:40,550
Yeah, it's a honeymoon, man.

228
00:08:40,550 --> 00:08:43,050
Woo!

229
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:45,060
(radio breaking up)

230
00:08:45,050 --> 00:08:47,050
(Tom chuckling)

231
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,560
We're havin' Ray
Bolger practice!

232
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,070
We're doing acting improv.

233
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:57,060
We're bacon!

234
00:08:57,070 --> 00:08:59,070
Bacon.

235
00:08:59,070 --> 00:09:00,070
Bacon.

236
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:02,570
Bacon.

237
00:09:04,570 --> 00:09:05,570
Bacon?

238
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,580
Meanwhile, on Trapper John.

239
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:12,080
Hey, maybe we'll
see Chad Everett.

240
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:13,080
Your telegrams.

241
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,590
Uh, thanks for
the horse, Mary Lou.

242
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:17,080
It's Consuela.

243
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:23,090
Directing her in there?

244
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:27,600
Hi.

245
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:31,100
Fred MacMurray wants
his slippers back.

246
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:32,600
More telegrams.

247
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:35,110
You think they'd be
out of paper by now.

248
00:09:35,100 --> 00:09:36,600
I wish you had toilet
paper in this room.

249
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:38,110
Can I get you anything?

250
00:09:38,110 --> 00:09:40,110
How 'bout a room with a view?

251
00:09:40,110 --> 00:09:41,110
Or a pretty woman?

252
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:42,110
I'm afraid there
are no windows

253
00:09:42,110 --> 00:09:43,610
in this wing of the hospital.

254
00:09:43,610 --> 00:09:46,120
Sunlight interferes with
post-radiation studies.

255
00:09:46,110 --> 00:09:48,110
Besides, you'll be well
enough to leave here soon.

256
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:49,620
Straight scoop?

257
00:09:49,620 --> 00:09:51,620
Double dip?

258
00:09:51,620 --> 00:09:53,120
(laughing nervously)
In the meanwhile,

259
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:54,630
there're all these
beautiful flowers.

260
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:57,120
They keep coming in
by the truckload.

261
00:09:57,130 --> 00:09:59,630
They're all poppies,
heh-heh-heh-heh.

262
00:10:01,130 --> 00:10:03,630
My God, you're ugly.

263
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:04,640
Well, how's our
number one patient?

264
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:06,130
Uh, he just went
number two, Doctor.

265
00:10:06,130 --> 00:10:08,130
Can't say I blame him.

266
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:10,140
How do you feel?

267
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:12,140
- You tell me.
- You're hot.

268
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:13,640
You're making
excellent progress.

269
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:15,150
Good, then I can
get outta here soon.

270
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:16,140
Soon?

271
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:19,140
It's not as though we're
a prison, you know.

272
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:21,150
What would you
call it, Dr. Revere?

273
00:10:21,150 --> 00:10:22,150
Call me Paul.

274
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:23,650
The two weeks I've been here,

275
00:10:23,650 --> 00:10:25,660
I haven't been allowed
outside this room.

276
00:10:25,650 --> 00:10:27,150
I can't have visitors,
use the telephone,

277
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:28,660
or read a newspaper.

278
00:10:28,660 --> 00:10:30,160
I've been cut off from
the outside world,

279
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,660
and you tell me it's
not like I'm in prison.

280
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,660
You've been through
a terrible experience.

281
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:35,670
But I'm still in one piece!

282
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:37,160
I can get around.

283
00:10:37,170 --> 00:10:38,170
I know, physically,
you're in better condition

284
00:10:38,170 --> 00:10:40,170
than we could've hoped, but.

285
00:10:40,170 --> 00:10:41,670
But, what?

286
00:10:41,670 --> 00:10:43,170
Emotionally.

287
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:44,680
You'll always
be a little girl.

288
00:10:44,670 --> 00:10:45,500
What about emotionally?

289
00:10:45,510 --> 00:10:47,010
It just takes time.

290
00:10:47,010 --> 00:10:48,510
You've been a member
of the space program

291
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:50,010
long enough to understand that.

292
00:10:50,010 --> 00:10:53,020
I don't know what
I understand anymore.

293
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:54,510
All I know is,

294
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:56,520
I haven't seen one familiar
face since I got back,

295
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:59,020
and you're as much a
stranger to me as anyone.

296
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:00,520
(Dr. Revere chuckling)
That's pretty good!

297
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:02,030
There are many people
in this end of the program

298
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:05,020
you've never come
in contact with.

299
00:11:05,030 --> 00:11:07,030
What about our flight surgeon?

300
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:10,030
Uh, we had to shoot 'im.
He had a broken leg.

301
00:11:10,030 --> 00:11:12,540
Dr. Bellman is dead, Neil.

302
00:11:12,530 --> 00:11:15,030
He died of a heart attack
while you were still in space.

303
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:18,040
We didn't wanna
disturb you with it.

304
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:20,040
I'm sorry.

305
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,550
Jell-O?

306
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:23,040
Look, I give you my word

307
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:24,040
that it won't be
long before you'll

308
00:11:24,550 --> 00:11:26,550
be reunited with
the outside world,

309
00:11:27,050 --> 00:11:29,050
and with your
family and friends.

310
00:11:29,050 --> 00:11:31,060
- You know, all that crap.
- We need you back in space.

311
00:11:31,050 --> 00:11:34,050
You're much too valuable
to be stuck in here.

312
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:36,060
In the meantime,

313
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:37,560
reports concerning your health

314
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:40,560
- are being let out.
- And so is your gown.

315
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:42,570
You know there are
people all over the world

316
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,560
concerned about your well-being?

317
00:11:44,570 --> 00:11:45,570
I told you, didn't I?

318
00:11:45,570 --> 00:11:47,070
The president himself
insisted upon being

319
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:49,570
the first one to speak
to you on the telephone?

320
00:11:49,570 --> 00:11:52,580
What, so all of a
sudden he's a cable show?

321
00:11:52,570 --> 00:11:54,570
And keep him informed.

322
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:58,580
(suspenseful music)

323
00:11:58,580 --> 00:12:00,080
Kinda fuzzy.

324
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:04,580
Steve Austin! (gasps)

325
00:12:04,590 --> 00:12:06,090
Sort of.

326
00:12:06,090 --> 00:12:07,590
Okay, we're ready to

327
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:09,090
lay in the background
vocals now.

328
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:12,590
(speaking in foreign language)

329
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:14,600
- I'm sorry.
- Bless you.

330
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:16,600
You don't speak
Russian, Doctor?

331
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:18,100
No.

332
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:20,600
I never had time to
learn another language.

333
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:23,610
Nice try, astro-spy.

334
00:12:23,610 --> 00:12:26,110
I smell a rat,
a big Commie rat.

335
00:12:26,110 --> 00:12:29,610
Can I have some
borscht, Dr. Stalin?

336
00:12:29,610 --> 00:12:33,120
You find these have
been helping you relax?

337
00:12:33,110 --> 00:12:37,110
Yeah, but I haven't
been sleeping well lately.

338
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:39,120
Now, uh, now the arm.

339
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:41,630
Keep having these
strange dreams.

340
00:12:41,620 --> 00:12:43,620
About the mission?

341
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:47,130
About everything that's
ever happened to me.

342
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:50,130
It's like I'm reliving my
life over again in my sleep.

343
00:12:50,130 --> 00:12:51,640
Even the smallest details.

344
00:12:51,630 --> 00:12:53,630
Only Cameron Mitchell's there.

345
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:56,640
And, I've got the feeling

346
00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:58,640
I've been talking
in my sleep, too.

347
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:00,140
And wetting.

348
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:02,150
Sorry.

349
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:05,140
Well, our nurses are
trained to be discreet.

350
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:07,150
Say, I think I'll
test that theory.

351
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:08,150
I think I told you that

352
00:13:08,150 --> 00:13:09,650
the fact that you survived,

353
00:13:09,650 --> 00:13:11,660
and your fellow
astronauts perished,

354
00:13:11,650 --> 00:13:14,150
that makes it all the
more traumatic for ya.

355
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:16,660
But these nightmares,
they'll pass.

356
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:19,160
So will your suspicions,
and your fears.

357
00:13:19,160 --> 00:13:20,160
And your kidney stones.

358
00:13:20,160 --> 00:13:22,170
You're getting
better, colonel.

359
00:13:22,160 --> 00:13:23,660
Just takes time.

360
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:25,670
The check's in
the mail. (chuckles)

361
00:13:25,670 --> 00:13:28,670
Oh, say, I've got
a surprise for you.

362
00:13:28,670 --> 00:13:31,180
You know a man
named Tony Angelo?

363
00:13:31,170 --> 00:13:33,670
Yeah, he owns a
restaurant in Boston.

364
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:36,180
Well, he's flying some
fresh scrod out here for you.

365
00:13:36,180 --> 00:13:38,680
Ooh, scrod? Eeuch.

366
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:40,180
Well, Tony oughtta know.

367
00:13:40,180 --> 00:13:41,690
He served me enough of it.

368
00:13:42,180 --> 00:13:43,680
(chuckles) Well,
with a little luck,

369
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:45,190
it'll be here for dinner.

370
00:13:45,190 --> 00:13:47,190
Speaking of
Boston, Dr. Revere.

371
00:13:47,190 --> 00:13:48,690
I'm not Russian!

372
00:13:48,690 --> 00:13:51,200
Are you any relation to Paul?

373
00:13:51,190 --> 00:13:53,690
- Paul Revere?
- And the Raiders?

374
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:54,700
No, I don't think so.

375
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,200
Oh, but I know Davy Jones.

376
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:57,200
Oh, doesn't matter.

377
00:13:57,200 --> 00:14:00,200
Paul Revere wasn't much
of a ballplayer anyway.

378
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:04,210
Oh, (chuckles) I'm afraid
I'm as ignorant of sports

379
00:14:04,210 --> 00:14:05,710
as I am languages.

380
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:08,210
And history and sociology and...

381
00:14:09,710 --> 00:14:14,220
(MST3K theme music)

382
00:14:17,750 --> 00:14:19,750
I think we should
stop for tonight.

383
00:14:20,260 --> 00:14:22,260
Watching this guy
sleep is making me tired.

384
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:25,760
Look, I'm his
father and I can.

385
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:26,760
Oh.

386
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:28,270
The more patient
we are with him now,

387
00:14:28,260 --> 00:14:29,760
the more cooperative
he'll be in the future.

388
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:31,260
Know what I mean?

389
00:14:31,270 --> 00:14:33,770
Doctor, do you really think

390
00:14:33,770 --> 00:14:35,770
that he believes he's home?

391
00:14:35,770 --> 00:14:38,280
He believes he's at a Motel 6.

392
00:14:38,270 --> 00:14:40,770
Well, I think he
has some doubts.

393
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:43,280
Well, I have some
doubts, too, Doctor.

394
00:14:43,780 --> 00:14:45,780
Especially about the way
you're handling this.

395
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:47,280
You think you're the
only one who hurts?

396
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,290
All these questions
about his past.

397
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:50,780
These questions have helped me

398
00:14:50,790 --> 00:14:52,290
to eliminate most
of his suspicions.

399
00:14:52,790 --> 00:14:54,790
Most, but not all.

400
00:14:54,790 --> 00:14:56,290
No, no.

401
00:14:56,290 --> 00:14:57,800
It's only a matter of time now

402
00:14:57,790 --> 00:15:00,790
before he figures what
we're doing to him.

403
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:02,800
But until then,

404
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:05,300
think of what we're learning
about their space program.

405
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:07,810
And, about dining in Boston!

406
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:10,300
(heart pounding)

407
00:15:10,310 --> 00:15:12,810
I can hear my heart beating.

408
00:15:15,810 --> 00:15:17,310
Let's dress 'im up.

409
00:15:17,310 --> 00:15:18,820
He's so cute, I
hate to wake him.

410
00:15:18,810 --> 00:15:20,310
My way is better.

411
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:23,320
(heart pounding)

412
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:27,830
You don't believe
that, do you, Doctor?

413
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:32,320
I believe you
can break him, yes.

414
00:15:32,330 --> 00:15:33,830
Bend him, mold him.

415
00:15:33,830 --> 00:15:35,830
There's always Ward E.

416
00:15:35,830 --> 00:15:40,840
But you also stand a chance
of breaking his mind as well.

417
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:42,840
And a mind is a
terrible thing to waste.

418
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:44,840
And breaking up is hard to do.

419
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:46,840
And mulch is good for your lawn.

420
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:52,840
We will continue the
questioning another hour.

421
00:15:52,850 --> 00:15:54,850
- Benedict.
- Arnold!

422
00:15:54,850 --> 00:15:57,350
I can't devote my
whole life to this man.

423
00:15:57,350 --> 00:15:58,860
This one man.

424
00:15:58,850 --> 00:16:01,350
Oh, he wants to
play the field, huh?

425
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,360
More important things.

426
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:05,360
Yeah, he's got a
Botany 500 ad to do.

427
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:06,360
Doctor.

428
00:16:12,370 --> 00:16:13,870
Another hour.

429
00:16:19,370 --> 00:16:21,370
Okay, let me check that.

430
00:16:21,380 --> 00:16:23,380
Scene three, I enter up left.

431
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:27,890
Attention all personnel.

432
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:29,880
Tonight's movie will
be Stranded in Space

433
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:31,380
starring Dorothy Lamour.

434
00:16:31,390 --> 00:16:34,390
Neil, let's talk
about Paul Revere.

435
00:16:34,390 --> 00:16:35,890
Well, the big
hook was costuming,

436
00:16:35,890 --> 00:16:38,400
but after Cherokee
People, they really...

437
00:16:38,390 --> 00:16:39,890
(machine beeping)

438
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:42,900
Drug's wearing off, Doctor.

439
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:45,400
Let 'im rest for tonight.

440
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:47,400
Give him another injection.

441
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:49,910
Too much o'that drug
is gonna kill 'im.

442
00:16:51,910 --> 00:16:53,910
Then maybe we could
all get some rest.

443
00:16:53,910 --> 00:16:55,410
This is a movie.

444
00:16:55,410 --> 00:16:57,920
This is a movie on drugs.

445
00:16:57,910 --> 00:16:58,910
Any questions?

446
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:07,420
That's for him!

447
00:17:14,930 --> 00:17:17,430
(Tom humming a lullaby)

448
00:17:20,940 --> 00:17:22,440
Ooh!

449
00:17:25,940 --> 00:17:27,950
Benedict.
Get me security.

450
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:29,440
And some eggs, some
Hollandaise sauce,

451
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:30,940
a toasted English muffin.

452
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:33,450
(exclaiming, punch landing)

453
00:17:35,450 --> 00:17:36,950
I think he's getting better.

454
00:17:37,450 --> 00:17:39,960
(alarm blaring)

455
00:17:42,460 --> 00:17:44,460
You have lovely skin.

456
00:17:44,460 --> 00:17:45,460
Hi-kee-ba.

457
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:47,970
Gotta go! (chuckles)

458
00:17:50,970 --> 00:17:51,970
(gun firing)

459
00:17:51,970 --> 00:17:53,470
Oh, man!

460
00:17:53,470 --> 00:17:55,470
I hope this hospital
treats gunshot wounds.

461
00:17:55,470 --> 00:17:56,470
And no shoes!

462
00:17:58,970 --> 00:18:00,470
Hey, where's he goin'?

463
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:01,980
Hey, he has to leave the
hospital in a wheelchair.

464
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:03,480
That's the law.

465
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:07,480
Oh, no hall pass.

466
00:18:07,480 --> 00:18:08,990
I've had dreams like this.

467
00:18:08,980 --> 00:18:10,480
I'm gun-shot and I'm
really late for class,

468
00:18:10,490 --> 00:18:11,990
and I don't have a hall pass!

469
00:18:11,990 --> 00:18:13,490
Oh, quick, in here.

470
00:18:13,490 --> 00:18:14,990
Mr. Fisher's science room!

471
00:18:16,990 --> 00:18:19,000
Switcher!

472
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Let's go buff our helmets, Bob.

473
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:28,510
That was close. Too close.

474
00:18:32,510 --> 00:18:35,510
That's it, I'll
dress as Murphy!

475
00:18:37,510 --> 00:18:39,520
Oh, brother, Murphy's a woman!

476
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:45,020
But it's his size.

477
00:18:46,020 --> 00:18:48,530
Who put the overalls
in Mrs. Murphy's locker?

478
00:18:50,530 --> 00:18:52,530
Uh, uh, I wanna paint you.

479
00:18:53,530 --> 00:18:54,530
Sort of.

480
00:18:55,530 --> 00:18:57,030
Don't be afraid.

481
00:18:57,030 --> 00:18:59,040
I won't hurt you.

482
00:18:59,030 --> 00:19:01,030
I just wanna spread
you on some toast.

483
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:04,040
I won't tell
anyone you're here.

484
00:19:05,540 --> 00:19:07,540
Help! He's in here! Help!

485
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:10,540
Yep, there she goes.

486
00:19:13,550 --> 00:19:15,050
He's in there, in the lab.

487
00:19:15,550 --> 00:19:16,550
I knew that.

488
00:19:16,550 --> 00:19:17,550
Outta my way.

489
00:19:20,050 --> 00:19:22,050
I want Rockford in my office!

490
00:19:22,060 --> 00:19:23,560
Oh, excuse me.

491
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:30,070
He's turned into a
little light, sneaky.

492
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:31,060
Basement.

493
00:19:34,570 --> 00:19:35,570
(synthetic chiming)

494
00:19:35,570 --> 00:19:36,570
Come in.

495
00:19:39,570 --> 00:19:40,570
I want Mannix.

496
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:49,090
This'll be great.

497
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:50,080
Watch this, Bob.

498
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,080
Steve, Al, you know what to do.

499
00:19:53,090 --> 00:19:54,590
Oh, yes, sir.

500
00:19:54,590 --> 00:19:56,090
We understand.

501
00:19:57,090 --> 00:19:58,100
Benedict.

502
00:19:59,590 --> 00:20:00,090
Fire.

503
00:20:00,090 --> 00:20:03,590
(guns firing)

504
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:05,600
Now we make 'im talk.

505
00:20:15,110 --> 00:20:17,610
(gasps) Oh, they shot
my spare turtlenecks.

506
00:20:17,610 --> 00:20:19,620
I think Harry-O is behind this.

507
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:22,620
We gotta go.

508
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:24,120
We gotta get outta here.

509
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,120
Oh, no, they killed Snuggles!

510
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:28,630
I can't believe that.

511
00:20:34,130 --> 00:20:39,140
(metal sliding and clanging)

512
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:43,540
No, no. I'm sorry.

513
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:45,540
- Oh, I'm afraid so.
- Oh, I'm afraid not.

514
00:20:45,540 --> 00:20:47,540
- You're high.
- Oh, you're high.

515
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:48,550
I know you are, but what am I?

516
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:50,040
(mockingly) I know
you are, but what am I?

517
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:52,040
Hey, hey, come on.

518
00:20:52,050 --> 00:20:53,550
What are you two little metal
monsters bickering about now?

519
00:20:53,550 --> 00:20:55,050
It's all Crow's fault.

520
00:20:55,050 --> 00:20:56,550
We were playing with our
Toppers TV tradin' cards

521
00:20:56,550 --> 00:20:58,560
and I wanted to maybe trade
'im my complete Love Boat set,

522
00:20:59,050 --> 00:21:00,550
which is worth a ton,

523
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:02,060
for his half-finished
Columbo villains,

524
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:04,060
and his crummy Name
O'the Game season two.

525
00:21:04,060 --> 00:21:05,060
And now he tells me

526
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:06,560
my Love Boat collection
is incomplete

527
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:08,570
'cause I gotta have a card
for each time a star appeared,

528
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:09,560
and I say he's high.

529
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:11,060
Oh, who's high?

530
00:21:11,070 --> 00:21:12,570
You know how much this Name
O'the Game pack is worth?

531
00:21:12,570 --> 00:21:15,070
I'm talkin' James
Farentino, Tony Franciosa.

532
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:16,070
Big deal.

533
00:21:16,070 --> 00:21:18,580
Hey, hold on.
Hold on now.

534
00:21:18,570 --> 00:21:21,570
I think I can give us a little
insight into this problem.

535
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:24,080
Tom, you only want his
Columbo villains set

536
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:26,080
because you've got
Cameron Mitchell,

537
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:27,080
and his value just went up,

538
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:29,090
'cause he's in today's movie.

539
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:30,080
Oh, I guess so.

540
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:32,080
Yeah, and Crow,
you're not much better,

541
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:35,590
tryin' to scam Tom's complete
Loveboat season one set.

542
00:21:35,590 --> 00:21:37,090
The set's not complete.

543
00:21:37,090 --> 00:21:38,100
Oh, it is too.

544
00:21:38,090 --> 00:21:39,590
Listen, I helped him
tape it together.

545
00:21:39,590 --> 00:21:41,590
Look it, his arms
are inoperable.

546
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:42,600
I gotta do it.

547
00:21:42,600 --> 00:21:44,600
See, all the cards go together

548
00:21:44,600 --> 00:21:47,100
to form ship's
doctor, Bernie Kopell!

549
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:48,110
See?

550
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:49,600
Dick-weed.

551
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:50,600
Hey, knock it off
Hey, Crow!

552
00:21:50,610 --> 00:21:51,610
Come on, what's wrong?

553
00:21:51,610 --> 00:21:53,110
Well, I'm sorry.

554
00:21:53,110 --> 00:21:54,110
I was just tryin' to play
hardball with ya, Servo.

555
00:21:54,610 --> 00:21:56,110
Come on, no hard feelings, pal.

556
00:21:56,110 --> 00:21:57,610
Okay. I guess not.

557
00:21:57,610 --> 00:21:58,620
Okay, I tell ya what.

558
00:21:58,610 --> 00:22:00,110
You can have the
whole Love Boat set,

559
00:22:00,110 --> 00:22:01,610
and I'll forget about
the other stuff,

560
00:22:01,620 --> 00:22:03,620
if you help me finish
my Sweathogs collection.

561
00:22:04,120 --> 00:22:06,120
Well, you can have
Gabe Kaplan for free.

562
00:22:06,620 --> 00:22:08,130
Oh, yuck, I don't
even want him.

563
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:09,620
I'm after Lawrence
Hilton-Jacobs.

564
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:11,120
Hey, well, listen.

565
00:22:11,130 --> 00:22:13,130
I've got somethin' that'll
really make your mouths water.

566
00:22:13,130 --> 00:22:16,130
The complete new
kids-in-court collection.

567
00:22:16,130 --> 00:22:17,630
That's right.

568
00:22:17,630 --> 00:22:21,640
Every major child star who's
been arrested, convicted,

569
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:22,640
or has litigation pending!

570
00:22:22,640 --> 00:22:23,640
Check it out!

571
00:22:23,640 --> 00:22:25,140
Wow.
Hey cool!

572
00:22:25,140 --> 00:22:27,640
Hey, Servo, look, there's
stats on the back.

573
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:29,150
Charges, trial
status, even lawsuits!

574
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:31,640
Yeah, look, there's Adam
Rich from Eight is Enough!

575
00:22:31,650 --> 00:22:34,150
Hey, the whole
Diff'rent Strokes gang!

576
00:22:34,150 --> 00:22:37,650
Dana Plato for holdin' up a
video store with a pellet gun.

577
00:22:37,650 --> 00:22:39,160
Allegedly, Crow.

578
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:40,150
That's right.

579
00:22:40,150 --> 00:22:42,150
There's Gary Coleman
for suing his parents.

580
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:43,660
Hey, wait a minute!

581
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:44,660
Todd Bridges was acquitted!

582
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:46,160
That card's worthless.

583
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:47,660
Hey, I know, but listen.

584
00:22:47,660 --> 00:22:49,170
I've got the complete set

585
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:50,660
now that I've got
Danny Bonaduce,

586
00:22:51,170 --> 00:22:53,670
who was arrested for
beating up a transvestite.

587
00:22:53,670 --> 00:22:55,670
Oh, wow, you got Bonaduce?

588
00:22:55,670 --> 00:22:57,170
Hey, where'd you
get all this anyway?

589
00:22:57,170 --> 00:22:58,680
Oh, I got 'em from Gypsy.

590
00:22:58,670 --> 00:23:00,170
Gypsy?

591
00:23:00,170 --> 00:23:01,670
She said all she had
were rabbi cards.

592
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:03,180
No, I just traded her
this Richard Basehart

593
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:04,680
from Voyage to the
Bottom of the Sea,

594
00:23:04,680 --> 00:23:06,680
and she gave me
the complete set.

595
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:08,690
- (exclaiming) What?
- Oh, wow.

596
00:23:08,680 --> 00:23:09,680
Gypsy, you come 'ere you rotten.

597
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:14,680
Tradin' is easy, once
you know the secret.

598
00:23:14,690 --> 00:23:16,690
I'm Marshall Brodien.

599
00:23:16,690 --> 00:23:20,700
(MST3K theme music)

600
00:23:23,970 --> 00:23:25,470
Richard Basehart?

601
00:23:25,470 --> 00:23:27,470
- I can't believe you, Gypsy.
- I got lucky.

602
00:23:27,470 --> 00:23:29,970
Here's where switchin'
to Sprint'll pay off.

603
00:23:29,970 --> 00:23:31,480
Yeah, no coin slot.

604
00:23:31,470 --> 00:23:32,970
This is a better world!

605
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:36,980
Now what was
that number again?

606
00:23:36,980 --> 00:23:39,980
(dial tone)

607
00:23:39,980 --> 00:23:41,990
Number, please.

608
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:43,480
(Joel stammering)

609
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:44,980
Number, please.

610
00:23:44,990 --> 00:23:46,990
A woman's secret
fantasy, please?

611
00:23:48,990 --> 00:23:50,490
I wanna call Cape Kennedy.

612
00:23:50,490 --> 00:23:52,000
Uh, the senator's

613
00:23:51,990 --> 00:23:54,490
doing some offshore
drilling right now

614
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:58,000
Cape Kennedy,
General RE Williams.

615
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:02,010
I have no listing
for a Cape Kennedy.

616
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:03,500
You've got to.

617
00:24:03,500 --> 00:24:05,500
It's a space center
outside of Orlando!

618
00:24:05,510 --> 00:24:08,010
I have a Tony Orlando,

619
00:24:08,010 --> 00:24:09,510
but that's the only one.

620
00:24:09,510 --> 00:24:11,010
It's in Florida!

621
00:24:11,010 --> 00:24:13,020
She was on Good Times.

622
00:24:13,010 --> 00:24:15,510
Oh, you mean Esther Rolle?

623
00:24:18,020 --> 00:24:22,530
Operator, you speak English.

624
00:24:22,520 --> 00:24:24,020
There's got to be a Florida.

625
00:24:24,030 --> 00:24:26,530
You're not dealing with AT&T.

626
00:24:26,530 --> 00:24:29,530
If you care to speak
to my supervisor.

627
00:24:29,530 --> 00:24:32,540
Your supervisor who?
Mr. Stalin?

628
00:24:32,530 --> 00:24:34,530
Man, phone service
is so much better

629
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:36,540
where I come from.

630
00:24:36,540 --> 00:24:40,540
(emulating Russian
accent) Nostrovia, comrade.

631
00:24:48,050 --> 00:24:51,560
(all exclaiming)

632
00:24:51,550 --> 00:24:54,550
Boy, that guy better
find a hospital.

633
00:24:54,560 --> 00:24:56,060
He's wounded.

634
00:24:57,560 --> 00:24:59,060
His pants fit good, though.

635
00:24:59,060 --> 00:25:01,060
Very nice cut.

636
00:25:01,060 --> 00:25:02,570
Haggars?

637
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:03,560
Fara.

638
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:05,060
For Guys, perhaps?

639
00:25:05,070 --> 00:25:06,570
Dad 'n' Lad.

640
00:25:07,070 --> 00:25:08,570
Sansabelt, I think.

641
00:25:13,070 --> 00:25:16,070
Here's someone
who can help me.

642
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:18,080
Uh-oh, that's Jame Gumb.

643
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:20,580
(emulating Gumb) It puts
the lotion on its skin.

644
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:22,590
or else it gets the hose again.

645
00:25:28,590 --> 00:25:29,590
Bye!

646
00:25:31,590 --> 00:25:32,590
Been out there long?

647
00:25:32,590 --> 00:25:34,590
Aw, few auditions,
Fruit of the Loom ad.

648
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:35,600
You know.

649
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:40,600
Oh, this your first
made-for-TV movie then?

650
00:25:44,610 --> 00:25:46,110
Chinese lanterns?

651
00:25:46,610 --> 00:25:48,110
So, where ya headed?

652
00:25:48,110 --> 00:25:49,610
Well, I was
headed for Florida,

653
00:25:49,610 --> 00:25:52,120
but I guess you ain't
got one o'those!

654
00:25:52,610 --> 00:25:53,610
Those moons.

655
00:25:53,610 --> 00:25:56,110
What about 'em?

656
00:25:56,120 --> 00:25:58,120
Do they always look like that?

657
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:00,620
Sure, if you're hammered.

658
00:26:00,620 --> 00:26:02,130
How do ya want 'em to look?

659
00:26:02,120 --> 00:26:05,620
Well, they're supposed to
look like Jackie Gleason!

660
00:26:08,130 --> 00:26:11,130
Third planet from the sun.

661
00:26:11,130 --> 00:26:14,640
There's only one moon.

662
00:26:14,640 --> 00:26:17,140
It's called Earth.

663
00:26:17,140 --> 00:26:19,140
Did you ever hear of it?

664
00:26:19,140 --> 00:26:20,640
Yeah, I'm not goin' that far.

665
00:26:21,140 --> 00:26:23,150
Nope, never did.

666
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:25,640
Well, I'm writing a
short story about it.

667
00:26:25,650 --> 00:26:28,650
Yeah, that's it.
Distant planet called Earth.

668
00:26:31,150 --> 00:26:33,660
Oh, that was exciting.

669
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:37,160
Whoa, whoa, hang on a minute!

670
00:26:37,660 --> 00:26:39,660
Something's wrong here.

671
00:26:40,160 --> 00:26:42,670
Stryker. Neil Stryker.

672
00:26:42,660 --> 00:26:44,660
He appears friendly.

673
00:26:44,670 --> 00:26:46,170
- Snuggles, get off me.
- In point of fact,

674
00:26:46,170 --> 00:26:49,170
he's unstable, paranoid,
extremely dangerous.

675
00:26:49,170 --> 00:26:52,180
And he's cute when he
sleeps, but you know that.

676
00:26:54,180 --> 00:26:58,180
A malignant disease
that must be eradicated.

677
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:00,180
With Eredicane?

678
00:27:00,180 --> 00:27:01,690
Dri-Plan? Lasso?

679
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:03,180
Somethin' by Monsanto.

680
00:27:03,680 --> 00:27:04,680
Roundup?

681
00:27:04,690 --> 00:27:07,690
(mechanical shade whirring)

682
00:27:09,690 --> 00:27:12,200
(chuckling) Hey, look!
They're in the Brady house!

683
00:27:12,190 --> 00:27:13,690
Is there a question?

684
00:27:13,690 --> 00:27:14,690
Yes.

685
00:27:14,700 --> 00:27:16,700
He looks just like us.

686
00:27:16,700 --> 00:27:18,200
I thought he'd be different.

687
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:19,700
Take my word for it.

688
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:22,210
Neil Stryker is different.

689
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:23,700
Stryker's a good cop!

690
00:27:25,210 --> 00:27:26,710
How many of us are
to know the truth

691
00:27:26,710 --> 00:27:29,210
about this man, Stryker,

692
00:27:29,210 --> 00:27:32,220
that, uh, he's a stranger
from another planet?

693
00:27:32,210 --> 00:27:34,210
Only those with
top-secret priority.

694
00:27:34,220 --> 00:27:36,720
Inner council members
such as yourself.

695
00:27:36,720 --> 00:27:37,720
For obvious reason,

696
00:27:37,720 --> 00:27:39,220
there was no need to
alarm the entire planet

697
00:27:39,220 --> 00:27:40,720
with the true facts of the case.

698
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:42,230
Oh, like Watergate.

699
00:27:42,220 --> 00:27:43,720
Um, sir, how come
the Landers sisters

700
00:27:43,720 --> 00:27:44,720
aren't at this meeting?

701
00:27:44,730 --> 00:27:46,730
What facts will they be given?

702
00:27:46,730 --> 00:27:48,230
They'll be told
to be on the lookout

703
00:27:48,230 --> 00:27:49,730
for an escaped mental patient,

704
00:27:49,730 --> 00:27:52,240
one considered
extremely dangerous.

705
00:27:52,230 --> 00:27:54,230
Yet witty and urbane.

706
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:56,240
- Yes?
- Young Orson Welles?

707
00:27:56,240 --> 00:27:57,740
Is he to be take alive?

708
00:28:00,240 --> 00:28:01,750
He's to be taken.

709
00:28:03,240 --> 00:28:04,740
That's all, thank you.

710
00:28:04,750 --> 00:28:06,250
Now, I'll be in my
office on Tuesday,

711
00:28:06,250 --> 00:28:07,250
but not Thursday.

712
00:28:07,250 --> 00:28:09,750
Have your textbooks and
your syllabus with you.

713
00:28:12,250 --> 00:28:14,250
Boy, you know how many
TV series'd be wiped out

714
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:16,260
if this room was blown up?

715
00:28:17,760 --> 00:28:19,760
Let me tell ya.

716
00:28:19,760 --> 00:28:21,770
Cameron, I just
wanted to say that

717
00:28:21,760 --> 00:28:23,260
that speech was sterling.

718
00:28:24,770 --> 00:28:25,770
I was moved.

719
00:28:25,770 --> 00:28:28,270
Stop reading over my shoulder.

720
00:28:31,270 --> 00:28:32,780
You? You got a problem?

721
00:28:32,770 --> 00:28:34,770
Talk to my TA.

722
00:28:34,780 --> 00:28:35,280
Yes?

723
00:28:35,780 --> 00:28:37,280
He could be captured alive.

724
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:39,280
Mmm-huh.

725
00:28:39,780 --> 00:28:41,280
Yes, Doctor, that's
a possibility.

726
00:28:41,280 --> 00:28:43,790
You could order it.

727
00:28:44,290 --> 00:28:47,290
Look out, here comes Jan!
Everybody scatter!

728
00:28:47,290 --> 00:28:49,290
You don't understand,
do you, Doctor?

729
00:28:49,290 --> 00:28:50,790
He's one man, alone.

730
00:28:52,790 --> 00:28:54,790
One man alone.

731
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:56,300
Go shoot yourself.

732
00:28:56,300 --> 00:28:58,300
Lemme tell you
something, Doctor.

733
00:28:58,300 --> 00:28:59,800
Before the Perfect Order,

734
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:03,310
the history books were filled
with stories of one man alone

735
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:06,800
that changed the entire
course of our great planet.

736
00:29:06,810 --> 00:29:09,810
One man alone wanting
to be dictator.

737
00:29:09,810 --> 00:29:12,320
One man alone
thinking he was God.

738
00:29:12,310 --> 00:29:17,310
One man alone feeling he knew
what was best for all mankind.

739
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:19,820
- Bush?
- Reagan?

740
00:29:19,820 --> 00:29:21,320
Each of these men
caused suffering--

741
00:29:21,820 --> 00:29:23,320
And a lotta bad theater.

742
00:29:23,320 --> 00:29:24,820
Yes, that's true.

743
00:29:24,830 --> 00:29:25,830
But this is different.

744
00:29:25,830 --> 00:29:27,330
Different? How different?

745
00:29:27,330 --> 00:29:28,830
He's given us proof
that intelligent life

746
00:29:28,830 --> 00:29:31,330
exists elsewhere
in the universe.

747
00:29:31,330 --> 00:29:33,840
Benedict, doesn't that
mean anything to you?

748
00:29:33,830 --> 00:29:35,830
Think what we can
learn from this man.

749
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:37,340
I am thinking, Doctor.

750
00:29:37,340 --> 00:29:39,340
Yeah, I thought I
smelled wood burning!

751
00:29:39,340 --> 00:29:42,350
(all chuckling)

752
00:29:43,340 --> 00:29:44,840
Our society works...

753
00:29:46,850 --> 00:29:48,850
Because we all
believe the same way.

754
00:29:48,850 --> 00:29:50,350
Just ask Werner Erhard.

755
00:29:50,350 --> 00:29:52,860
We all feel alike.

756
00:29:52,850 --> 00:29:54,350
We all dedicate ourselves

757
00:29:54,360 --> 00:29:57,360
to what's best for
the Perfect Order.

758
00:29:57,360 --> 00:29:59,860
And in turn, the Perfect
Order does what's best for us.

759
00:29:59,860 --> 00:30:01,830
And then someone comes along

760
00:30:01,830 --> 00:30:02,800
And doesn't pay union dues.

761
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:05,300
Someone with very
different ideas.

762
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:06,800
With very destructive ideas.

763
00:30:06,800 --> 00:30:09,310
Do you know what I've learned
from this man, Doctor?

764
00:30:09,300 --> 00:30:10,300
Oh, you're talkin' to me?

765
00:30:10,800 --> 00:30:11,800
I've learned that he's cursed

766
00:30:11,810 --> 00:30:13,810
with an absolute
freedom of thought.

767
00:30:14,310 --> 00:30:16,310
He's cursed with an
inquisitive nature,

768
00:30:16,310 --> 00:30:17,810
an uncontrollable spirit.

769
00:30:17,810 --> 00:30:19,320
Oh, he's not
gonna sing, is he?

770
00:30:19,310 --> 00:30:22,310
Much the same way most
of our planet was cursed,

771
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:24,320
before the Perfect Order.

772
00:30:24,320 --> 00:30:25,320
Burger, fries, and a shake.

773
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:28,320
And now, Doctor, he's
walking freely in our streets.

774
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:29,830
In our midst.

775
00:30:29,820 --> 00:30:32,820
Oh, believe me, Doctor.

776
00:30:32,830 --> 00:30:34,330
Today, one man.

777
00:30:35,330 --> 00:30:37,330
Tomorrow, 10.

778
00:30:38,830 --> 00:30:40,830
In a week, a thousand.

779
00:30:40,830 --> 00:30:42,330
Do you think I
look like Shatner?

780
00:30:42,840 --> 00:30:44,340
In a month, an epidemic.

781
00:30:45,840 --> 00:30:47,340
In two months, two epidemics.

782
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:48,850
Believe that?

783
00:30:48,840 --> 00:30:50,840
No, but it was a nice speech.

784
00:30:56,350 --> 00:31:00,360
Doctor, I'm asking
if you believe it.

785
00:31:00,350 --> 00:31:01,850
I do, I do.
I do believe in spooks.

786
00:31:01,860 --> 00:31:02,860
I do, I do, I do.

787
00:31:02,860 --> 00:31:03,860
I.

788
00:31:03,860 --> 00:31:05,360
Do you believe it?

789
00:31:05,360 --> 00:31:06,360
Or. Not.

790
00:31:07,860 --> 00:31:09,370
Yes.

791
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:11,860
Do you further believe he
must be dealt with quickly?

792
00:31:11,870 --> 00:31:12,870
Once and for all?

793
00:31:13,870 --> 00:31:15,370
Yes.

794
00:31:15,370 --> 00:31:17,370
Do you believe
in little pixies?

795
00:31:17,370 --> 00:31:20,380
Then we have no
quarrel, have we, Doctor?

796
00:31:20,370 --> 00:31:21,870
No, but I'm gonna
whip your butt

797
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:22,880
in racquetball later.

798
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:23,880
No.

799
00:31:27,880 --> 00:31:28,890
(smooching sound)

800
00:31:28,880 --> 00:31:30,380
He needed that.

801
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:34,890
Alice, can you
make me a sandwich?

802
00:31:34,890 --> 00:31:36,390
Great macrame.

803
00:31:38,390 --> 00:31:40,400
You know, this
bookcase used to slide.

804
00:31:40,390 --> 00:31:43,890
Henry, you think he was being
completely honest with me?

805
00:31:43,900 --> 00:31:46,400
(chuckling) Nope.

806
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:47,400
Yes.

807
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,410
How quickly some forget.

808
00:31:50,400 --> 00:31:51,400
How quickly.

809
00:31:51,410 --> 00:31:53,410
Nice encyclopedias.

810
00:31:54,910 --> 00:31:57,910
The suffering
Henry, suffering.

811
00:31:57,910 --> 00:32:00,920
Did you ever see
anyone starve to death?

812
00:32:00,910 --> 00:32:02,410
What'd you have in mind?

813
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:03,420
No, sir.

814
00:32:03,420 --> 00:32:05,420
No, of course not.

815
00:32:05,420 --> 00:32:06,920
You moron.

816
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:09,930
It's not a pretty sight.

817
00:32:09,920 --> 00:32:13,420
Something you never will forget.

818
00:32:13,430 --> 00:32:15,930
Jeez, I'm just a temp.

819
00:32:18,930 --> 00:32:19,930
Henry...

820
00:32:22,940 --> 00:32:24,940
Dr. Revere is lying to us.

821
00:32:26,440 --> 00:32:28,440
He's taken Stryker's side,

822
00:32:28,440 --> 00:32:32,450
which only proves how dangerous
a man like Stryker is.

823
00:32:34,450 --> 00:32:38,450
I think Dr. Revere needs a rest.

824
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:42,460
What do you think, Henry?

825
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:44,960
Have him eliminated,
permanently.

826
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:47,460
- Ward E?
- Yes, I think, Ward E.

827
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:48,460
[Tom] Woody?

828
00:32:48,460 --> 00:32:49,970
Woody?

829
00:32:51,970 --> 00:32:55,470
(emulating Jame Gumb)
Now put the lotion
on your skin.

830
00:32:58,970 --> 00:33:00,470
Boy, you know, I'm just crazy

831
00:33:00,470 --> 00:33:02,470
about these split-level
Tudors around here.

832
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:04,980
They're great.

833
00:33:04,980 --> 00:33:05,980
Really snappy.

834
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:07,480
Funny how space looks

835
00:33:07,480 --> 00:33:09,490
- a lot like Sacramento.
- Yeah.

836
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:11,480
Or Ojai.

837
00:33:12,990 --> 00:33:14,490
Smoke?

838
00:33:14,490 --> 00:33:15,990
Hey, you saved the coupons?

839
00:33:15,990 --> 00:33:17,990
Sure, how do you think
I got this swell truck?

840
00:33:17,990 --> 00:33:19,000
Hey!

841
00:33:26,500 --> 00:33:28,500
Hurt your hand?

842
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:30,010
What?

843
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:31,500
The only time I ever see
people use their right hands

844
00:33:31,510 --> 00:33:33,010
is when they've hurt their left.

845
00:33:33,010 --> 00:33:35,510
Oh, the unclean hand.
Use it all the time.

846
00:33:35,510 --> 00:33:38,010
Sprained my wrist.
It's still a little sore.

847
00:33:38,010 --> 00:33:39,520
Happened to me,
I'd starve to death.

848
00:33:39,510 --> 00:33:41,010
It's exactly like our world,

849
00:33:41,010 --> 00:33:43,010
except they're all left-handed!

850
00:33:43,020 --> 00:33:44,020
Wow, I get it.

851
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:45,520
I know what you mean.

852
00:33:45,520 --> 00:33:48,520
Takes some getting used to.

853
00:33:48,520 --> 00:33:50,030
(music blaring from radio)

854
00:33:50,520 --> 00:33:52,020
Ah, and so does that.

855
00:33:52,030 --> 00:33:53,030
Ah, it's a left-handed radio

856
00:33:53,030 --> 00:33:56,030
and a left-handed orchestra.

857
00:33:56,030 --> 00:33:58,530
Heavy-handed.

858
00:33:58,530 --> 00:34:00,040
Mmm-huh.

859
00:34:00,030 --> 00:34:01,030
Radio Moscow here.

860
00:34:01,030 --> 00:34:03,530
Boy, is that guy square.

861
00:34:03,540 --> 00:34:06,040
With a capital square.

862
00:34:07,540 --> 00:34:10,050
Hey, let's put somethin'
different on the tunage--

863
00:34:10,040 --> 00:34:13,540
Senior Deputy of
the inner council.

864
00:34:13,550 --> 00:34:15,050
To work for the Perfect Order

865
00:34:15,550 --> 00:34:18,050
is to live in harmony
and peace with oneself.

866
00:34:18,050 --> 00:34:22,060
To live in harmony and
peace is man's highest goal.

867
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:23,060
Page two?

868
00:34:23,060 --> 00:34:24,560
To work for the Perfect Order

869
00:34:24,560 --> 00:34:26,560
is to live in harmony
and peace with oneself.

870
00:34:26,560 --> 00:34:28,560
Man, I hate it when
people sing along.

871
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:30,070
To live in harmony and peace

872
00:34:30,060 --> 00:34:33,060
is man's highest goal.

873
00:34:33,070 --> 00:34:35,070
Before our morning
wake-up exercises,

874
00:34:35,070 --> 00:34:36,070
we have a special bulletin

875
00:34:36,070 --> 00:34:38,570
from the Department
of Protection.

876
00:34:38,570 --> 00:34:40,080
A mental patient--
(Crow dramatically coughing)

877
00:34:40,070 --> 00:34:41,570
I'll get off right here.

878
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:43,580
Has escaped from
the medical center.

879
00:34:43,580 --> 00:34:45,580
Well, this is as
far as I'm goin'.

880
00:34:45,580 --> 00:34:47,580
I wanna thank you again
for stoppin' for me.

881
00:34:47,580 --> 00:34:49,090
Not many people would've.

882
00:34:49,080 --> 00:34:51,080
- Yeah, well.
- Thanks again!

883
00:34:51,080 --> 00:34:53,580
Stryker may have been wounded.

884
00:34:53,590 --> 00:34:57,590
Stryker is considered
extremely dangerous.

885
00:34:57,590 --> 00:35:02,500
(MST3K theme music)

886
00:35:12,870 --> 00:35:15,870
Oh, at least they've
got a Barnes & Noble.

887
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,380
(mockingly) Yes?

888
00:35:26,390 --> 00:35:28,390
May I help you?

889
00:35:28,390 --> 00:35:29,890
Uh, not without
hurting yourself.

890
00:35:29,890 --> 00:35:30,890
I mean, what?

891
00:35:30,890 --> 00:35:32,400
You looking for
something special?

892
00:35:32,390 --> 00:35:34,390
Uh, yeah, I'm lookin'
for the new one,

893
00:35:34,390 --> 00:35:35,390
American Psycho?

894
00:35:35,400 --> 00:35:37,400
Do you have any
books about our planet?

895
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:38,900
About Terra?

896
00:35:39,900 --> 00:35:40,900
- Yeah.
- Sure.

897
00:35:41,400 --> 00:35:42,910
About where we are
in relation to the sun

898
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:44,900
and the other planets.

899
00:35:44,910 --> 00:35:46,410
I'm afraid you'd have to go

900
00:35:46,410 --> 00:35:48,410
to one of the technical
bookshops for that.

901
00:35:48,410 --> 00:35:51,410
Perhaps some of those
near the university.

902
00:35:51,410 --> 00:35:53,920
If you're interested in
the history of Terra,

903
00:35:53,910 --> 00:35:56,410
I've got several
very good books here.

904
00:35:56,420 --> 00:35:58,920
Let's see, over here,
Portnoy's Complaint.

905
00:35:58,920 --> 00:36:00,420
This is most complete,

906
00:36:00,420 --> 00:36:02,430
from the beginning, right
to the present time.

907
00:36:02,420 --> 00:36:05,420
Oh, wait, isn't that
where Scarlett O'Hara lived?

908
00:36:09,430 --> 00:36:11,430
You have any
with more pictures?

909
00:36:11,430 --> 00:36:14,940
Yeah, and anything
not by Isaac Asimov?

910
00:36:14,940 --> 00:36:17,440
So, it goes back
about 35 years?

911
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:18,440
That's right.

912
00:36:18,440 --> 00:36:20,440
But you said,
from the beginning.

913
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:23,950
Yes, from the beginning
of the Perfect Order.

914
00:36:23,940 --> 00:36:26,440
Oh, of course, Terra
had history before that.

915
00:36:26,450 --> 00:36:29,450
You're too young
to remember that.

916
00:36:29,450 --> 00:36:32,460
It had its bad times and good.

917
00:36:32,450 --> 00:36:34,950
People seemed to laugh a
lot more in those days.

918
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:36,460
I was a woman then, too.

919
00:36:36,460 --> 00:36:38,960
There seemed to
be music everywhere.

920
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:41,960
I remember the
concerts in the park.

921
00:36:41,960 --> 00:36:43,970
Children pulled
apart like fresh bread.

922
00:36:43,960 --> 00:36:45,960
Don't get me wrong.

923
00:36:45,970 --> 00:36:47,970
Things are much better now.

924
00:36:47,970 --> 00:36:50,470
The Perfect Order
has given us dignity.

925
00:36:50,470 --> 00:36:52,980
There's no war
anymore, no starvation.

926
00:36:52,970 --> 00:36:55,470
We all live together
in peace and harmony.

927
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:58,980
There's nothing prior
to the Perfect Order

928
00:36:58,980 --> 00:37:01,980
worth recording or
even remembering.

929
00:37:01,980 --> 00:37:03,490
'Cept the Beatles.

930
00:37:03,480 --> 00:37:05,980
You're welcome to
browse around if you wish.

931
00:37:05,990 --> 00:37:08,990
You won't find any obscene
reading material in this shop.

932
00:37:08,990 --> 00:37:10,490
Then, why bother?

933
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:19,500
Got to try and make sense!

934
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,510
I thought that was a
book you couldn't put down.

935
00:37:26,010 --> 00:37:27,510
Oh, can I do anything?

936
00:37:27,510 --> 00:37:29,010
Got any Tama Janowitz?

937
00:37:29,010 --> 00:37:30,510
No, I'll be all right.

938
00:37:30,510 --> 00:37:31,510
I've a room upstairs

939
00:37:31,510 --> 00:37:34,020
if you'd care to rest
for a little while.

940
00:37:34,010 --> 00:37:37,010
No one would disturb you.
I'm quite alone here.

941
00:37:37,020 --> 00:37:38,520
Really?

942
00:37:38,520 --> 00:37:40,020
That's very kind.

943
00:37:41,020 --> 00:37:43,530
Do you think you can make it?

944
00:37:43,520 --> 00:37:45,020
Yes, thank you.

945
00:37:45,030 --> 00:37:47,030
His name isn't Yoda,
by any chance, is it?

946
00:37:47,030 --> 00:37:50,530
(oboe-based instrumental music)

947
00:37:50,530 --> 00:37:51,530
Never mind the oboe.

948
00:37:51,530 --> 00:37:54,040
That always follows
me around, you know.

949
00:37:58,540 --> 00:38:01,540
It's small, but comfortable.

950
00:38:01,540 --> 00:38:04,050
You can make yourself
some tea in there.

951
00:38:04,040 --> 00:38:05,540
And there's fresh bread.

952
00:38:05,550 --> 00:38:07,050
Or hooch.

953
00:38:07,050 --> 00:38:09,050
Well, I think I'd just
like to rest a little while.

954
00:38:09,050 --> 00:38:10,550
Well, whatever you want.

955
00:38:10,550 --> 00:38:13,560
Uh, turn the Magic
Fingers on if you'd like.

956
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:17,060
Man, is this ever great!

957
00:38:17,060 --> 00:38:20,060
I've got a place to live
and a brand-new dad!

958
00:38:20,060 --> 00:38:22,570
Now I'll trash the room.

959
00:38:22,560 --> 00:38:24,060
Well, maybe not.

960
00:38:32,570 --> 00:38:35,070
Well, he'll be perfect
for my experiment.

961
00:38:35,080 --> 00:38:37,080
You know, uh, that's
not a Tiffany lamp

962
00:38:37,580 --> 00:38:39,580
It's a Debbie Gibson lamp.

963
00:38:39,580 --> 00:38:41,580
- Oh!
- I'm kidding.

964
00:38:41,580 --> 00:38:42,590
Oh, hello, Mother?

965
00:38:42,580 --> 00:38:44,580
I've found myself
a new roommate.

966
00:38:44,580 --> 00:38:46,580
Medical assistance, please.

967
00:38:46,590 --> 00:38:50,590
Oh, great, a planet
full o'tattletales.

968
00:38:50,590 --> 00:38:52,600
Where's Burt Convy?

969
00:38:53,590 --> 00:38:55,590
District 1-1-4.

970
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:58,100
Yes, that's
right, the old man.

971
00:38:58,100 --> 00:38:59,100
In the sweater.

972
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:00,600
Yes, I'll wait.

973
00:39:00,600 --> 00:39:02,100
There's nothing else I can do.

974
00:39:02,100 --> 00:39:03,610
Oh, that's exciting.

975
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:05,100
For cryin' out loud.

976
00:39:05,110 --> 00:39:07,540
Last week, on McCloud.

977
00:39:09,040 --> 00:39:12,050
I think the exercise worked.

978
00:39:12,050 --> 00:39:15,050
Is that a diaper over there?

979
00:39:19,050 --> 00:39:20,560
(door opening)

980
00:39:21,050 --> 00:39:23,050
He's in there.

981
00:39:24,560 --> 00:39:26,060
I saw you weren't feeling well.

982
00:39:26,060 --> 00:39:28,060
I phoned medical assistance.

983
00:39:28,060 --> 00:39:31,070
They've sent a doctor over.

984
00:39:31,060 --> 00:39:33,060
Uh, you got a straight razor?

985
00:39:33,070 --> 00:39:35,070
I wanna shave my wrists, yeah.

986
00:39:38,570 --> 00:39:40,580
Oh, quick, give
myself a haircut.

987
00:39:40,570 --> 00:39:42,570
Trim the sideburns.

988
00:39:42,580 --> 00:39:45,080
Splash on some Canoe.

989
00:39:48,080 --> 00:39:49,080
Oh, great.

990
00:39:52,090 --> 00:39:55,590
Well, if it isn't little
Miss I-Won't-Tell-a-Soul.

991
00:39:58,590 --> 00:40:00,100
(emulating
female) I trust you.

992
00:40:00,090 --> 00:40:01,590
You've got to listen to me.

993
00:40:01,590 --> 00:40:07,590
(Crow mumbles in muffled voice)

994
00:40:07,600 --> 00:40:09,610
Bite my hand once for yes.

995
00:40:12,610 --> 00:40:15,110
I'm an astronaut
from another planet.

996
00:40:16,110 --> 00:40:19,110
- A planet called Earth.
- Yes, of course you are.

997
00:40:19,110 --> 00:40:22,120
We were returning home
from a long mission and,

998
00:40:22,120 --> 00:40:24,120
somehow, entered your
gravitational field,

999
00:40:24,120 --> 00:40:26,120
and were brought down.

1000
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:27,620
Mmm-huh. Go on.

1001
00:40:27,620 --> 00:40:29,120
When I came to, I
was at the hospital,

1002
00:40:29,120 --> 00:40:31,630
and the people there were
tryin' to convince me

1003
00:40:31,620 --> 00:40:33,120
I was back on Earth.

1004
00:40:34,130 --> 00:40:35,630
Now, you must've heard something

1005
00:40:36,130 --> 00:40:37,130
about an alien spacecraft
being recovered.

1006
00:40:37,130 --> 00:40:40,140
It's not something
that happens every day.

1007
00:40:41,630 --> 00:40:43,630
I heard that there was, uh,

1008
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:47,140
a special patient in room 420.

1009
00:40:47,140 --> 00:40:48,640
That's him, get it?

1010
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:51,650
And that Dr. Revere was
in charge of the case.

1011
00:40:51,640 --> 00:40:53,140
That's all?

1012
00:40:53,150 --> 00:40:55,150
If I'd needed to know
more, they would've told me.

1013
00:40:55,150 --> 00:40:56,650
'Cause they're perfect.

1014
00:40:56,650 --> 00:41:00,660
But I do know that Dr.
Revere is a very fine man.

1015
00:41:00,650 --> 00:41:03,150
He wouldn't do
anything to hurt you.

1016
00:41:03,160 --> 00:41:05,160
Dr. Revere kept me
drugged and a prisoner.

1017
00:41:05,160 --> 00:41:07,160
There, see, I told you
they were good people.

1018
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:09,670
You must be mistaken.

1019
00:41:10,160 --> 00:41:13,160
Some patients do confuse
treatment with punishment.

1020
00:41:13,170 --> 00:41:15,170
The lucky ones.

1021
00:41:15,170 --> 00:41:16,670
So, you're telling me,

1022
00:41:16,670 --> 00:41:18,670
all he wants to do is help me.

1023
00:41:18,670 --> 00:41:19,680
Yes.

1024
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:24,180
I'm not buyin' Trojans
with you dressed like that.

1025
00:41:24,180 --> 00:41:25,680
And quit followin' me!

1026
00:41:25,680 --> 00:41:28,180
Spread out. Come on,
we look conspicuous.

1027
00:41:28,180 --> 00:41:30,690
After all, you're
a very important man.

1028
00:41:30,680 --> 00:41:32,680
And when you've
completely recovered,

1029
00:41:32,690 --> 00:41:35,190
you'll be treated
as an honored guest.

1030
00:41:35,190 --> 00:41:36,190
With savage beatings.

1031
00:41:36,690 --> 00:41:37,690
A welcome visitor to Terra.

1032
00:41:38,190 --> 00:41:41,200
And I'm sure they'll be able
to help you get back to...

1033
00:41:41,190 --> 00:41:44,690
Earth, if you'll only
return to the hospital.

1034
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:47,700
They won't hurt you.

1035
00:41:48,200 --> 00:41:49,200
I promise.

1036
00:41:51,200 --> 00:41:52,200
Ha!

1037
00:41:53,210 --> 00:41:54,210
All right.

1038
00:41:54,210 --> 00:41:55,710
On one condition.

1039
00:41:55,710 --> 00:41:57,710
We stop at the Dairy Queen.

1040
00:41:57,710 --> 00:41:58,710
That you drive me there.

1041
00:41:59,210 --> 00:42:00,220
I wanna turn myself
into Dr. Revere.

1042
00:42:00,210 --> 00:42:02,710
And I wanna turn
myself into Ivana Trump.

1043
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:06,720
(foreboding music)

1044
00:42:11,720 --> 00:42:15,720
(all three vocalizing)

1045
00:42:20,230 --> 00:42:21,730
Yes, yes, it worked!

1046
00:42:21,730 --> 00:42:24,730
I'm gonna score on an
interplanetary scale!

1047
00:42:31,240 --> 00:42:33,240
TV movie.

1048
00:42:33,250 --> 00:42:35,250
Mission: Impossible music.

1049
00:42:35,250 --> 00:42:37,250
Hey, it's the Doublemint Twins!

1050
00:42:39,750 --> 00:42:42,750
What are they
shooting through, gauze?

1051
00:42:47,260 --> 00:42:49,260
That's right, you
can arrive on New Terra

1052
00:42:49,260 --> 00:42:52,770
in style in the new Plymouth
Fury, left-hand drive option.

1053
00:42:54,270 --> 00:42:56,270
You know, they shoot a lotta
Quincy scenes on this road.

1054
00:42:56,770 --> 00:42:58,770
And, up there, that's
where Columbo lived.

1055
00:43:03,780 --> 00:43:05,280
Um, I'd like to have
children right away

1056
00:43:05,280 --> 00:43:06,780
- if it's okay with you.
- Turn left.

1057
00:43:06,780 --> 00:43:08,280
Could we pull over first?

1058
